I'll take a crack at it. Everyone has different playing styles.

One common thread in my three ascensions so far and other games that
go deep into the dungeon is that I quickly got my AC down. Usually this
is a combo of a wish for dragon scale and buying protection from the 
priest. BTW displacement or invisibility enhances your AC because it
causes monster to ignore, or to miss you.

Resistances are critical. Reflection, Magic resistance, Poison resistance, 
and then the others gained by crowning greatly assist in moving the game
forward.

Another theme that frankly I've been expoliting is pudding farming. If you
can get a character to a point where they have the high ground on an altar
and get a pudding on the level, it equates to virtually unlimited hit points,
protection, and loot. The fact of the matter is that sheer boredom is the
only balance to pudding farming. I've seriously started thinking that
there needs to be some controls put in place. Something like a game of life
reduction due to overcrowding, less item drops, and a god that
"is bored with your sacrifice!" in order to curtail rampant pudding farming.

Another one is failing to realize that it's a turn based game. Most deaths
that are not early game are stupid ones. Stuff like picking up cockatrice
corpses without gloves (did that my last game!) or having the drawbridge fall
on you at the castle. Or the time that I got green slimed with the Healer
Quest Artifact Staff (which cures sliming) in hand.  Or simply failing to
stop in picthed melee to heal and not tracking HP. Once you remember that the
game stops as soon as you stop giving it input, then you can stop and
possibly prevent stupid deaths.

One thing that I still think can help a lot of players (as I think it helped
me) is to play pacifist for awhile. Novice players tend to think that Nethack
is about killing stuff. The real goal is ascension, and many times to do that
you have to avoid situations that can damage that chance. Being pacifist
teaches you that you don't always have to fight. You can run away.  You can
let your pet deal with it. You can use a trap. Use can use Elbereth.  Many
times you die simply because you let too many monsters whack away in melee
when you don't need to melee at all.

Another is falling into the management traps. Nethack is about managing food, 
weight, power points, hit points, experience points and the like. I'm
ruthless about staying unencumbered. A blessed bog of holding is essential
gear.

Finally simply knowing how things work is helpful. I'm an info sponge. In
my last game I had my first crack at Master Kaen. I read all the guides and
posts I could find on the subject. Turned out not to be too difficult.
Same with alchemy, the use of blessed items, and artifacts. Find out
everything you can and then apply that information as best you can.

If you want to see, try stuff in wizmode. Equip a character to -50AC, MR,
reflection, invisibility/displacement and 100HP. Then simply wander the
dungeon avoiding everything and see what happens. You'd be surprise at
how far you can get when you're properly equipped. Then you work to get
that equipped in a real game. And trust me none of the items on the list
above are too terribly difficult to accomplish, especially when you start
with highly enchanted dragon scale and protection.

Good luck,

